.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt PING 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ping
.Nd send ICMP echo requests to network hosts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl afnoqv
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl i Ar wait
.Op Fl l Ar preload
.Op Fl m Ar ttl
.Op Fl p Ar pattern
.Op Fl S Ar src_addr
.Op Fl s Ar packetsize
.Op Fl t Ar timeout
.Op Fl W Ar waittime
.Op Fl z Ar tos
.Ar host
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends ICMP echo requests to
.Ar host ,
a name or an IPv4 address, once a second, and reports each echo reply:
its size, sender, sequence number, time to live and round trip time.
A reply seen twice is marked
.Ql (DUP!) .
When the count is reached, the time is up, or
.Nm
is interrupted
.Pq Dv SIGINT ,
it prints how many requests went out and how many replies came back, and
the least, average and greatest round trip time and its standard deviation.
.Pp
Each request carries
.Ar packetsize
data bytes, of which the first 8 hold the time it was sent and the rest a
pattern, which each reply is checked against.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Ring the bell with each reply.
.It Fl c Ar count
Stop after
.Ar count
replies, or once
.Ar count
requests have gone out and the last has had time to be answered.
.It Fl f
Flood: send a request a hundred times a second, printing
.Ql \&.
for each request and a backspace for each reply.
Only the superuser may use it.
.It Fl i Ar wait
Wait
.Ar wait
seconds, which may be a fraction, between requests; 1 by default.
Only the superuser may make it less than a second.
.It Fl l Ar preload
Send
.Ar preload
requests at once before the usual ones.
Only the superuser may use it.
.It Fl m Ar ttl
The time to live of the requests.
.It Fl n
Numeric output: addresses are always shown as numbers.
.It Fl o
Stop after the first reply.
.It Fl p Ar pattern
Fill the data with up to 16 bytes, given as hexadecimal digits, repeated.
.It Fl q
Quiet: print only the first line and the statistics.
.It Fl S Ar src_addr
Send from the address
.Ar src_addr .
.It Fl s Ar packetsize
Send
.Ar packetsize
data bytes, 56 by default (64 bytes of ICMP).
Fewer than 8 leave no room for the time, so no round trip time is shown.
.It Fl t Ar timeout
Stop after
.Ar timeout
seconds, however many replies have come.
.It Fl v
Verbose: also report the other ICMP packets received.
.It Fl W Ar waittime
Wait at most
.Ar waittime
milliseconds for each reply: a later one is counted but not shown.
It is also how long
.Nm
waits after the last request when no reply has come at all (10 seconds by
default).
.It Fl z Ar tos
The type of service of the requests.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
needs a raw socket, then gives up the privileges it was run with.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
At least one reply came.
.It 2
Requests went out but no reply came.
.It 64
The command line was wrong.
.It 68
The host could not be found.
.It 71
A system call failed.
.It 77
An option was used that only the superuser may use.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Three requests to the loopback interface:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ping -c 3 127.0.0.1
PING 127.0.0.1 (127.0.0.1): 56 data bytes
64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=0 ttl=64 time=0.060 ms
64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=1 ttl=64 time=0.058 ms
64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=2 ttl=64 time=0.064 ms

--- 127.0.0.1 ping statistics ---
3 packets transmitted, 3 packets received, 0.0% packet loss
round-trip min/avg/max/stddev = 0.058/0.061/0.064/0.002 ms
.Ed
.Pp
Whether a host answers within five seconds, in a script:
.Dl $ ping -q -o -t 5 10.0.2.2 > /dev/null && echo up
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr icmp 4 ,
.Xr inet 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
command appeared in
.Bx 4.3 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh BUGS
OxideBSD's IP layer does not yet act on
.Fl m ,
.Fl S
and
.Fl z :
requests go out with the stack's own time to live (64), type of service,
and the source address of the route to
.Ar host .
